A very good game with a very good port.  What's the issue with it?  It's drastically different from your other Final Fantasy titles.
Storyline;
The end of the Lightning Saga, wrapping up the Fabula Nova Crystallis universe. And man, what an ending! The storyline is kind of spread thin; the bulk of the storyline is on the Final Day. In order to unlock the Final Day, you need to gain Eradia, the bulk of which is gained from saving the souls of some familiar friends. It's a very hit-or-miss storyline; I can see how people might not like it, but for me, it's one of my most favorite stories of all time.
Gameplay;
This is where the drastic changes come in. While the previous FF13 games were more relatable to turn-based games, Lightning Returns is more relatable to action games.  Some fights require you to move around the field in order to strike a specific part of an enemy.
You have 3 "schemata" each with their own ATB gauges. Using actions require different amounts of ATB, with your other schemata's ATB gauges recharing while they aren't currently in use.  There are some action elements, like Perfect Guards and Perfect Attacks, where you perform guards/attacks on a specific frame in order to reduce damage to 0/increase damage.
Almost immediately from the start, the game becomes open-world.  While you spend the first 15-20 minutes or so in Luxerion, you're soon given free reign over the 4 different regions; enemies are all relative in strength for the first 7 days.  After the 7th day, they get modifiers to their HP, Attack, and Magic stats.
Difficulty;
It may just be because I'm terrible at action games, but even on normal mode, I found this to be the most challenging Final Fantasy game i've played.  One of the story bosses in The Wildlands means business, it took me an hour or two to find the right setup.
The superbosses are an insane challenge.  Aeronite in particular - you've got to take off 8-9m health within 3 minutes.  The first few times I attempted him, I was barely able to take off 3m before he took off.  I ended up completely cheesing it with a whole lot of ethers.
Music;
I may not have been listening too closely, because I didn't catch much, if any, songs unique to Lightning Returns.  It uses a mix of music from FF13 and FF13-2.  Luckily only the good tracks from 13-2!

Salvation Prophecy is an older but ambitious game, and is in my opinion a diamond in the rough. I did a few playthroughs with different races and it's clear that a lot of love went into this game. There are parts that definitely could've used more polish, but Salvation Prophecy is still an excellent mix of space combat and ground missions.
The game takes you between space and ground combat, both of which have offense and defense missions. There are primarily squad-based missions for the factions, but the main plot often has the player on a solo mission to explore a mysterious planet. There are also a chain of solo space missions for bounty-hunting. Later, as the player advances ranks within their faction, they can buy upgrades for their ship and eventually assume control of their faction. This lets the player order fleet and ground missions as well as build new buildings and space stations on conquered planets.
--(
) Space combat. While space stuff could be repetitive, it was overall very fun. The jump and wormhole mechanics made travel more interesting. It was an awesome feeling, being able to single-handedly take down an enemy capital ship and space station in the same mission once my squad had been killed.
--(
) The ability to go from exploring my faction space station, jump in a ship, fly to another planet, land on it, meet new and interesting monsters, and kill them, in a 3rd person perspective. We seriously need more games like that.
--(
) Ship upgrades. I love games that let me upgrade vehicle stuff, and my only complaint here was that there weren't more choices or options.
--(
) Some of the Salvation Prophecy storyline missions involve collecting runes with special powers that give the game a different twist beyond the usual sci-fi trope of simply getting bigger/better guns. Unleashing these runes on the very next ground mission was fun.
--(
) While all factions have mostly decent dialogue with occasional fun, the Wyr faction in particular has some great moments here.
--(
) Ground combat. It often seemed a bit clunky and the races felt poorly balanced. For example in most ground missions, fighting the humans or killer robots was easy, but fighting against the blue all-female race that could steal life energy as a ranged attack was much harder (however
as the all-female race was a blast).
--(
) Would've liked to see more mission types beyond a simple offense/defense on space and ground, and the bounty hunter chain. The prophecy plotline took care of this sometimes, but still could've used more variety in the faction missions.
--(
) The interior layout of the space stations was exactly the same across all factions. While the stuff inside the space station was somewhat different for each faction, it seemed a bit unrealistic that they'd all have the same map, with the exact same NPC jobs in each place. As such, once you know where your weapon vendor is for Race A, it will be in the exact same spot for the others. Definitely could have used more dev time to make the layouts feel unique to each faction.
--(
) Controls occasionally felt clunky. Worst was unstable wormholes, though maybe that was the entire point there.

